On Tuesday, December 31, 2013 12:46:04 PM UTC-5, Terrence Jorgensen wrote:
>
>
> On Monday, December 30, 2013 10:25:26 AM UTC-5, Eric Green wrote:
> >
> > fit <- cfa(f, data=input, ordered=names(input))
> >
> > I get an error that "system is computationally singular". When I run qr(input)$rank, I get a value of 26, which I believe means I have the expected number of linearly independent column vectors.
> >
> > If qr(input)$rank == dim(input)[2], why am I getting the "system is computationally singular" error? Or am I misunderstanding something?
> >
> >
> qr() assumes you have numeric (basically continuous, interval) data, not categorical.  You are treating the data (appropriately) as categorical when you run lavaan.  The fact that your your rank == 26 means none of the 2-way contingency tables should show evidence redundancy among any pairs of variables.  But I'm not sure where in the "system" the problem occurs.  The more complete error message might give a better clue for Yves to spot something, seeing as it is apparently some object (matrix?) named "B" that can't be inverted:
>
> Error in solve.default(B) :
>   system is computationally singular: reciprocal condition number = 1.12493e-16
>
>
>  Terry
